 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfT5b8epfiy/",
 "title": "How I Negotiated a Salary Hike",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator shares how she asked her CTO for a 33% salary hike after working at an EdTech startup for over a year without an increase. She explains the three things that helped her succeed and how she ended up with 7.2 LPA plus an 80,000 bonus.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator joined as a developer in 2018 with a 6 LPA salary at an EdTech startup.",
  "By August 2019, she had not received any hike, so she reached out to her CTO, Sriram, to schedule a salary discussion.",
  "She asked for a 33% increase, moving from 6 lakhs (6 LPA) to 8 LPA.",
  "The creator claims the norm in major companies is around a 10% raise, and it is uncommon to get more than a 30% raise in your first company or the same company.",
  "Three things that helped her: 1) audacity and confidence to reach out and get people to the discussion table, 2) having performance pointers ready (reducing student complaints after moving to a new scripting language with her team, working on GRE and TOEFL, and being onboarded onto admissions counseling), 3) negotiation over two to three rounds of discussions.",
  "She finally agreed to an offer of 7.2 lakhs plus an 80,000 bonus at the end of one year."
 ],
 "content": "Comment hike, If your appraisal is coming up and you’re hoping your manager will notice all the extra work you’ve done, listen to what I did in 2019. It is frustrating to not receive a hike even after completing more than a year in an organization. So, I joined as a developer in 2018 with a 6 LPA salary at an EdTech startup. And by 2019 August, I did not receive any hike. So, I decided to reach out to Sriram, my CTO and tell that I want to have a salary discussion and please arrange a meeting. So, after two to three days, I had a conversation. And I had asked for a 33% increase, which is from 6 lakhs, I wanted 8 LPA. Now, uh it is not very common for organizations to give more than 30% raise in your first company or in the same company. Nowadays, the norm in major companies is that you get around 10%. So, three things that helped me were: number one is the audacity and the confidence that with which I went and reached out and got people onto the table for that discussion. Number two is I had my pointers ready, that after I joined and we moved to this new scripting language, of course, along with my team members, we have reduced the number of complaints that come with come from the students and I have worked on GRE and TOEFL. I was already onboarded onto the admissions counseling. Now, number three is negotiation. There were two to three rounds of discussions. Finally, they had agreed to 7.2 lakhs and 80,000 bonus will be given at the end of one year. Now, I was happy with what was offered and I was really glad that I had made this conversation. Now, if you want to know how I have approached and if this would help you, comment hike and I'll send you the entire guide.",
